      <trans-unit id="help.current_pace.answer">
        <source>help.current_pace.answer</source>
        <target>Where your driving habits lead.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.current_pace.what">
        <source>help.current_pace.what</source>
        <target>These are the kilometres you drive right now per day, week and month. Polaris looks at your whole lease and gives more weight to the last 30 days, so a change in your habits shows here within a few weeks.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.current_pace.use">
        <source>help.current_pace.use</source>
        <target>Compare it with “From now on”: if this card is higher, you are on course to go over your allowance.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.budget.answer">
        <source>help.budget.answer</source>
        <target>What you can still drive.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.budget.what">
        <source>help.budget.what</source>
        <target>These are the kilometres per day, week and month that use up your allowance exactly at the end of the lease: the kilometres you have left, spread over the time you have left.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.budget.use">
        <source>help.budget.use</source>
        <target>They go up when you drive less and down when you drive more. Stay at or below them and you will not pay for extra kilometres.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.even_pace.answer">
        <source>help.even_pace.answer</source>
        <target>Your allowance, spread evenly.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.even_pace.what">
        <source>help.even_pace.what</source>
        <target>These are the kilometres per day, week and month your contract allows on average, from the first day of the lease to the last. They never change, whatever you drive.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.even_pace.use">
        <source>help.even_pace.use</source>
        <target>Use them as the yardstick for the other two cards.</target>
      </trans-unit>
