      <trans-unit id="help.common.periods">
        <source>help.common.periods</source>
        <target>Each row is a period: today (since your midnight), yesterday (the whole day), then the last 7, 14 and 30 days, counted from your midnight. They all end at your latest reading, which can be a few hours old. Before the first reading of the day, the first row says “No reading yet today”.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.common.today">
        <source>help.common.today</source>
        <target>Polaris counts the distance between two readings in the hours you usually drive (see “Driving hours” in the lease terms). Without them it spreads that distance evenly over the whole day, night included, so a reading at 21:00 gives part of the next morning to the day before.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.common.live">
        <source>help.common.live</source>
        <target>The snapshot of today is not saved yet, because Polaris saves it at midnight. The row is then compared with a projection rebuilt just now: what the midnight job would have saved. Nothing is stored from it.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.common.fallback">
        <source>help.common.fallback</source>
        <target>If even that is not possible, the row is compared with the latest earlier record and says “Since yesterday” or “Since N days ago”.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.compare_budget.answer">
        <source>help.compare_budget.answer</source>
        <target>Did you stay within your budget?</target>
